course: money
lesson: 20
title: Wages, salary and where family money comes from
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most family money comes from work: a salary is a fixed amount paid every month, so if Dad earns £2,000 a month, that is 12 × £2,000 = £24,000 a year, and it pays for the home, food and everything else.
explain: Some people are paid a wage for each hour: Jack's mum earns £12.50 an hour in a café, so an 8-hour shift brings £100, and more shifts mean more money; jobs at nights or weekends often pay more per hour.
explain: Before pay reaches the bank, tax and pension money is taken off: Dad's payslip says he earned £2,500 but £2,000 arrived, because £500 went on tax, which pays for schools, hospitals and roads; what is left is called take-home pay.
explain: Families get money in different ways, and all of them are normal: some people work for themselves and earn more some months than others, older people live on a pension, people between jobs can get help from the government, and a parent caring for a baby at home does real work that is just not paid.
fact: A salary is a fixed amount paid for a job, usually every month.
fact: A wage is pay for each hour or each week worked; fewer hours means less money.
fact: A shift is a set time at work, such as a day shift or a night shift.
fact: Jobs at unsocial hours, like nights and weekends, often pay more per hour because fewer people want those hours.
fact: Tax and pension payments are taken from pay before it arrives; what is left is called take-home pay.
fact: A payslip is a paper or screen showing what you earned and what was taken off.
fact: Self-employed people work for themselves; their income can go up and down from month to month, so they save in good months for slow ones.
fact: A pay rise is more money for the same job, but bills often go up too.
fact: Looking after children and a home is real work; it is unpaid, but it saves the family money and matters a lot.
fact: A pension is money paid to people after they retire, built up from what they paid in while working.
fact: When people lose their job, the government can give some money, called benefits, to help pay for basics; there is nothing to be ashamed of in this.
fact: Some people have two part-time jobs to earn enough, which means long and tiring days.
fact: Payday is the day pay arrives; many families paid monthly have to make money last until the next payday.
exercise: Dad earns a salary of £2,000 a month. How much is that in a year? | £24,000 | £20,000 | £2,400 | £12,000
exercise: What is a salary? | A fixed amount paid for a job, usually each month | Money given by a grandparent | Money found in a jar | A bill for electricity
exercise: Jack's mum earns £12.50 an hour. How much for an 8-hour shift? | £100 | £96 | £80 | £20.50
exercise: What is a wage? | Pay for each hour or week worked | Money paid to you after you retire | A tax you pay on all your shopping | Money borrowed from a bank each month
exercise: Dad earned £2,500 but £2,000 came into the bank. How much was taken off? | £500 | £4,500 | £250 | £2,000
exercise: Where did that money go? | On tax and pension, which pay for things like schools and later life | To the payslip printer | Someone stole it from the bank | Back to Dad's boss as a gift
exercise: Kai's mum earns £16 an hour on days and £20 on nights. How much more per hour do nights pay? | £4 | £36 | £6 | £2
exercise: Why do night shifts often pay more? | Fewer people want to work those hours | Nights are longer than days, so there's more work | Hospitals have more money to spend at night | Night workers work fewer hours than day workers
exercise: Aoife's da earned £3,000 in March and £1,000 in April. What is the average per month? | £2,000 | £4,000 | £1,500 | £3,000
exercise: Mum's pay went from £1,800 to £1,900 a month. How big was the pay rise? | £100 | £3,700 | £1,000 | £190
exercise: Leo's mum looks after his baby sister at home. Is that a job? | Yes, it is real work, just not paid | No, because nobody pays her anything for it | Only if she wears a proper uniform to do it | Only on weekdays, never at the weekend or holidays
exercise: Grandad's pension is £800 a month. How much is that in a year? | £9,600 | £8,000 | £960 | £8,800
exercise: Mo's dad earned £1,600 a month and now gets £800 of help while he looks for work. What fraction is that? | Half | A quarter | A third | Double
exercise: Priya's mum works 4 hours at £13 an hour at the school. How much is that? | £52 | £17 | £48 | £56
exercise: She then works 3 hours at the shop at £14 an hour. How much does she earn in the whole day? | £94 | £52 | £42 | £81
exercise: Theo's family has £70 left for the last 7 days before payday. How much is that a day? | £10 | £7 | £14 | £63
exercise: What is a pension? | Money paid to people after they stop working | Money for children's pocket money from the government | A type of bank card | Money paid for a part-time job
test: Ruby sees "Earned £2,500, take-home £2,000" and shrieks "who nicked the rest?!" What's the answer? | Nobody; £500 went on tax and pension | The bank kept it as a fee for looking after the money | Dad's boss forgot to pay it | It went on Dad's lunches
test: Ben yells "pizza night!" when Mum gets a £100 pay rise. Mum: "One pizza, then the rest goes on the gas bill." Why? | Bills often go up too, so a rise doesn't stretch far | Pizza is a need and gas is a want | Pay rises must be spent on pizza | The gas bill is always exactly £100
test: Mo's dad lost his job and says calmly, "I'm getting some help from the government till I find a new one." A kid at school sneers. What's true? | Getting help between jobs is normal and nothing to be ashamed of | Only lazy people ever lose jobs | Help from the government is never allowed | Mo's dad will never work again
test: Leo asks "does Mum even have a job?" Dad says "it's a job, just not a paid one." If a childminder cost £80 a day, what is a 5-day week of that care worth? | £400 | £80 | £85 | £160
test: Grandad: "I paid in for 45 years, now it pays me back, lad." What is he talking about? | His pension | His pocket money | A loot box | His mortgage
test: Aoife's da, a builder, says "some months it's grand, some months it's bleedin' quiet." ("Grand" means fine.) What should a self-employed person do? | Save in good months for the slow ones | Spend everything in the good months while you can | Stop working in quiet months and rest instead | Borrow money every month to keep things even
test: Jack's mum: "more shifts, more money, love." She works 3 shifts of 8 hours at £12.50. How much? | £300 | £100 | £240 | £37.50
test: Kai's mum, a nurse on nights: "pays a bit more, but I'm knackered." ("Knackered" means very tired.) What's the trade-off? | More pay against being very tired | More pay against having fewer patients to see | Less pay against getting more sleep at night | No trade-off at all, because nights pay the same
test: Theo's dad: "The week before payday, we're always counting pennies." Why? | Monthly pay has to last the whole month, so money gets tight near the end | The bank closes for a week every month | Pennies are worth more before payday | He just likes counting coins
test: What would happen if a family paid monthly spent most of its pay in the first week? | Money would run very short before the next payday | The next payday would come sooner | The bank would add more money free when it ran low | Nothing, pay lasts however you spend it
test: Isla's mate reckons "money just comes out the hole in the wall, innit." ("Hole in the wall" means a cash machine.) Where does it really come from? | From the family's bank account, filled mostly by pay from work | The machine prints it for anyone | The bank gives it free to families | It's left there by the government each morning
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a "salary"? | A fixed remuneration for employment, ordinarily payable monthly | A payment made by the state upon retirement | A charge levied upon the purchase of goods | A sum advanced by a bank subject to repayment
exam: It is given that a monthly salary of two thousand pounds is received. Compute the aggregate received over one year. | Twenty-four thousand pounds | Twenty thousand pounds | Two thousand four hundred pounds | Twelve thousand pounds
exam: It is given that gross earnings of two thousand five hundred pounds give rise to a payment into the bank of two thousand pounds. Ascertain the sum deducted, and identify its principal destination. | Five hundred pounds, applied chiefly to taxation and pension | Five hundred pounds, retained by the bank as a fee | Four thousand five hundred pounds, applied chiefly to taxation | Two hundred and fifty pounds, retained by the employer
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning financial assistance from the government during unemployment. | It is a lawful provision to meet basic needs and is no cause for shame | It is unlawful and must be repaid with interest | It exceeds the salary previously earned | It is available only to persons who have retired
